## IPv6
IPv6 Example: 2001:4860:4860::8888

They're represented using hexadecimal:
There are eight groups:

2001
0db8
85a3
0000
0000
8a2e
0370
7334

Each group represents 16 bits:
16 × 8 = 128 bits

| Feature               | IPv4                 | IPv6                                           |
| --------------------- | -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |
| Version               | 4                    | 6                                              |
| Address size          | **32-bit**           | **128-bit**                                    |
| Address format        | Decimal              | Hexadecimal                                    |
| Example               | `192.168.1.25`       | `2001:db8::1`                                  |
| Address groups        | 4 octets             | 8 groups                                       |
| Separator             | `.`                  | `:`                                            |
| Address space         | ~4.3 billion         | ~3.4 × 10³⁸                                    |
| Broadcast             | Yes                  | No                                             |
| Multicast             | Yes                  | Yes                                            |
| Anycast               | Limited/less central | Built in                                       |
| ARP                   | Used                 | **Not used**                                   |
| Neighbor discovery    | ARP                  | **NDP/ICMPv6**                                 |
| Address configuration | DHCP/manual          | SLAAC/DHCPv6/manual                            |
| Header                | More complex         | Simplified                                     |
| Fragmentation         | Routers can fragment | Source handles fragmentation                   |
| NAT                   | Very common          | Generally unnecessary for address conservation |

With IPv4:
IP address
     ↓
ARP
     ↓
MAC address

For example:

192.168.1.20
      ↓
"What MAC address has 192.168.1.20?"
      ↓
ARP
      ↓
AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF

Pv6 doesn't use ARP.
Instead, IPv6 uses Neighbor Discovery Protocol (NDP), which operates using ICMPv6.
So:
IPv4
IP → ARP → MAC
IPv6
IP → NDP/ICMPv6 → MAC

                 INTERNET
                    |
              Public IPv4
                    |
                 Router
              192.168.1.1
             /     |      \
            /      |       \
     192.168.1.20  .21     .22
       Laptop      Phone   Chromebook

# Note:
IPv4 and IPv6 can co-exist:
IPv4 → disappears → IPv6
             COMPUTER
             /      \
            /        \
         IPv4        IPv6
           |            |
           v            v
       IPv4 network   IPv6 network

This is called dual stack.
A computer can have:

IPv4:
192.168.1.25

IPv6:
2001:db8::25
at the same time.

